{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>A treemap finds the culprit once — CI stops it every time after</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Reading a treemap by hand is a debugging skill for when a regression has already landed. Once you've found one culprit pattern (a library with a heavy default export, an accidentally-duplicated dependency), it's worth writing a rule that catches that specific pattern automatically next time, rather than re-diagnosing it from scratch.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A per-PR bundle size budget in CI catches the regression before merge, but only a treemap (or a diff of the per-module breakdown) tells you which import to revert or fix — a budget check that just fails with "bundle exceeded 300 KB" hands the investigation right back to you.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Watch for the same module appearing twice in the treemap at different sizes — that's usually two different versions of the same transitive dependency, resolved separately because of how your package manager's dependency tree collapsed (or didn't). Deduplicating that (a lockfile fix, a resolutions/overrides entry) often recovers more size than any single import-path fix.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

